# @clara/web — public config. NEXT_PUBLIC_* is exposed to the browser;
# NEVER put a secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* var, and never commit a real value
# here — copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill it there.

# Supabase Auth + PostgREST — cookie-session auth (lib/supabase/client.ts,
# lib/supabase/server.ts, proxy.ts) and the HUMAN governance lane later lanes
# build against (Accept-Profile/Content-Profile: clara). Invite-only: there is
# no self-serve signup, so these two values are the entire CLIENT-side surface
# — the anon/publishable key is publishable by design, and NO SERVICE-ROLE KEY
# EVER REACHES THE BROWSER (docs/plan/active/frontend-handoff-2026-08-23.md
# §0.4, §4.3 — "nothing connects to Postgres directly and no service-role key
# ever reaches the browser").
#
# TRUED 2026-08-30 BY P4-4. The sentence above used to read "the service_role
# key is NEVER used in this app", which stopped being true when the invite mail
# courier landed: `app/api/invite/route.ts` (via lib/members/invite-mail.ts) is
# a SERVER-ONLY Route Handler that needs the service key to mint the Supabase
# half of the invite link. The invariant that actually matters is unchanged and
# is what this comment now states — the key never reaches a browser, and the
# variable carrying it is never `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed. See SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
# below.
#
# Named ANON_KEY to match this repo's existing apps/dashboard/.env.example
# convention. Supabase's current dashboard/docs call the same value the
# "publishable key" (new key format, `sb_publishable_...`) — either key format
# works here; createBrowserClient/createServerClient just take a string.
#
# THE VALUE'S CLASS IS ENFORCED AT BUILD TIME. `pnpm --filter @clara/web build`
# runs `scripts/check-public-key.mjs` before bundling and REFUSES to build
# unless this slot holds an `sb_publishable_...` key or a legacy JWT whose
# decoded role is positively `anon`. A secret key, a `service_role` JWT, a
# malformed value or an empty slot all stop the build — `next build` inlines
# whatever is here into every browser bundle, and the variable's NAME proves
# nothing about its value (security review 2026-08-27, finding 7).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INVITE MAIL COURIER (P4-4) — SERVER ONLY, all three. `app/api/invite/route.ts`
# reads them through lib/members/invite-mail.ts's `inviteMailCapability`, which
# is FAIL-CLOSED: with any one of them unset the courier answers 503
# `mail_not_configured` and CREATES NOTHING. That ordering is deliberate —
# `clara.invite_member` hands its plaintext token to its caller exactly once
# (`0147:418-423`), so an invite minted when no mail can go out is permanently
# unusable AND blocks that email for seven days behind CLR10 'an invite is
# already pending for this email'. NONE of these may ever gain a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix: `next build` inlines those into every browser bundle.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Supabase service-role key, used for EXACTLY ONE THING: `auth.admin.generateLink`
# ({type: "invite"}), which mints the `hashed_token` the invite link's path
# segment carries and which `verifyOtp` consumes on the accept page. It sends no
# email itself — the courier composes and sends the mail, because the 2026-08-30
# ruling puts Clara's OWN invite token in the link as `?ct=…` and no Supabase
# email template can carry it without persisting the plaintext in
# `auth.users.user_metadata` (see lib/members/invite-mail.ts's header).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Resend, the mail provider named by the ClaraBook handoff
# (docs/plan/active/frontend-handoff-2026-08-23.md:33). Called over its plain
# HTTPS API, so no npm dependency and nothing that fails on Cloudflare Workers.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From header on the invitation, e.g. `Clara <invites@yourdomain>`. Resend
# refuses a sender on an unverified domain, which surfaces to the admin as
# `mail_failed` with a CORRELATION ID — never the provider's own message. The
# courier stopped relaying upstream text entirely (independent review of #455,
# MEDIUM-3): Resend is handed the full secret invite URL, so its error strings
# have been in the same process as both bearer factors. The real, classified
# failure is on the SERVER LOG under that id, so a misconfigured sender is
# diagnosed from the log line rather than from the banner.
INVITE_MAIL_FROM=

# The Clara runtime, chat/SSE ONLY (lib/clara/api.ts's `runtimeBase()` — chat
# sessions/turns/streaming go DIRECT cross-origin from the browser when this is
# set, bypassing a Worker/serverless body-size cap). Document intake/bytes do
# NOT read this any more (independent review 2026-08-27, F1/F2 — see
# CLARA_RUNTIME_URL below): a browser-exposed NEXT_PUBLIC_* value is baked into
# the bundle at BUILD time, which is exactly wrong for a same-origin proxy that
# must read its destination at request time.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLARA_RUNTIME_URL=

# The Clara runtime, SERVER-SIDE ONLY — app/api/runtime/[...path]/route.ts's
# same-origin proxy for document intake (begin/bytes/finalize) and the
# document-bytes evidence viewer. NEVER NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed: this value must
# stay server-only and be read at REQUEST time, not baked into the browser
# bundle at build time (the same F1/F2 finding above). Unset → the proxy
# answers a typed 503 (`runtime_not_configured`), never a silent localhost
# fallback.
CLARA_RUNTIME_URL=
